uustat on OS-9 -- what was changed==================================Helge Oldach's `uustat', Hamburg 1989, from the Microware OS-9 archive.  Itreports on the UUCP job queue -- what is waiting, for which system, queued bywhom -- and can kill or rejuvenate a job.  It is the status-and-control halfof the UUCP set this disk already carries (uucico, uuclean, uucp, uulog,uuname, uuxqt).NOTHING WAS CHANGED.  It compiled as it came.ORIG/ holds the release as the archive had it: uustat.c, the author'sMakefile, and uustat.prf -- his manual page, written for Microware's `format'rather than for nroff, which is why it opens with `.fill' and `.header'rather than `.TH'.  It ships as DOC/uustat/uustat.prf.TWO THINGS WORTH KNOWING.1.  It links os9lib.  The author's Makefile says so -- `-l=/dd/LIB/os9lib.l'    -- and it is TOP Munich's library, which this collection carries as    GNULIB/os9lib.l.  Only one header of that library is needed at compile    time, <info.h>, and a copy sits beside the source: naming the whole of    DEFS/os9lib on the include path instead pulls in its <stat.h>, which    collides with the one already there ("multiple definition ... dev_t").2.  The spool directory is asked for, not assumed.  It calls    info_str("UUCP.SPOOL", ...) first and falls back to "/h0/SPOOL/UUCP"    only when that answers nothing.  There is no info database on this disk,    so the fallback is what you get; the collection's own spool is    /dd/SPOOL/uucp.  DOC/DEPENDS lists the fallback for what it is.Run bare it prints `uustat: warning: uucp is possibly active' and stops --that is it finding no job queue to report on, not a fault.  `uustat -?'prints the whole option list.